  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\n18z/1pm surface analysis shows a sprawling ridge of high\npressure from Quebec to the Gulf Coast. Meanwhile a 992mb low \nis tracking across northern Manitoba with its trailing cold \nfront extending southward into the Plains. Despite high clouds\nstreaming across the region from the northwest, partial \nsunshine has heated the dry airmass quite efficiently this \nafternoon...resulting in current temperatures in the lower to \nmiddle 70s. As has been the case for the past several days, \nreadings will quickly drop after sunset...with overnight lows \nranging from the lower to middle 40s in the Wabash River Valley \nto around 50 degrees along/west of the Illinois River.\n\nThe Manitoba low will continue to track eastward over the next\n24 hours, gradually pulling the cold front southward toward\nIllinois. However as the boundary becomes parallel with the \nupper flow, it will lose its southward momentum and remain well\nW/NW of the KILX CWA. As a result, the stretch of very warm and\ndry weather will continue through the middle of next week as\nhighs routinely climb into the middle to upper 70s while\novernight lows drop into the upper 40s and lower 50s.\n\nA second short-wave trough and its associated surface low \ntraversing southern Canada will push another cold front\nsouthward toward the region by the end of the week. With all 12z\nOct 24 models showing a slight weakening of the upper ridge, it\nappears this boundary will drop a bit further south than the\nfirst. With GFS precipitable water values edging up to 1.25-1.50\nimmediately along/ahead of the approaching front, have added 20\nPoPs for showers to the forecast Thursday and Thursday night\nalong/north of a Macomb to Minonk line. The front will gradually\ndissipate and/or lift back northward on Friday, resulting in a \nreturn to warm/dry conditions across the board.",
